
\section {Motivation}
\label{sec:motivation}

To date, two types of T2K oscillation analyses exist \cite{OurRun1and2Analysis2F, NueRun123Analysis}:
\begin{itemize}
\item
  $\nu_{e}$-appearance analyses,
  in which a joint determination of  $\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$ and $\delta_{CP}$ is performed
  exploiting the characteristics of the observed single e-like ring event sample.
  In this analysis, the values of  $|\Delta m_{32}^{2}|$ and $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ are fixed
  and a neutrino mass hierarchy is assumed.
\item
  $\nu_{\mu}$-disappearance analyses, 
  in which a joint determination of  $|\Delta m_{32}^{2}|$ and  $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ is performed
  exploiting the characteristics of the observed single $\mu$-like ring event sample.
  In this analysis, the values of  $\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$ and $\delta_{CP}$ are fixed
  and a neutrino mass hierarchy is assumed.
\end{itemize}


However, the $\nu_{\mu}$$\rightarrow$$\nu_{\mu}$ and $\nu_{\mu}$$\rightarrow$$\nu_{e}$ oscillation probabilities and, therefore, 
the characteristics of both the single e-like ring and  single $\mu$-like ring event samples,
depend on all the oscillation parameters.
This dependence of the oscillation probabilities
on parameters which are typically fixed is not negligible, and examples are shown in Fig.\ \ref{fig:oscprob}.
In this figure, the oscillation probability $P(\nu_{\mu}$$\rightarrow$$\nu_{\mu})$ 
is plotted for three different values of $\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$ with fixed values of $\left(\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}, |\Delta m_{32}^{2}|, \delta_{CP}\right)$,
while the oscillation probability $P(\nu_{\mu}\rightarrow\nu_{e})$ 
is plotted for three different values of  $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ with fixed values of  $\left(\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}, |\Delta m_{32}^{2}|, \delta_{CP}\right)$.


Assumptions made by the stand-alone analyses when fixing values of oscillation parameters can have a significant effect on the confidence regions obtained.
This is shown vividly in Fig.\ \ref{fig:effectpriornuecontours} for the case of the stand-alone $\nu_{e}$-appearance analysis, which 
typically uses a fixed value of $|\Delta m_{32}^{2}|$ and maximal 23-sector mixing ($\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ = 1). 
The figure compares the best-fit values and the 68\% CL and 90\% CL confidence regions (constructed with the constant-$\Delta\chi^{2}$ method) 
for $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ = 1 with those for $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ = 0.96. 

On the basis that the $\nu_{\mu}$$\rightarrow$$\nu_{e}$ oscillation probability is given, to first order, by
\begin{equation}
\displaystyle
  P(\nu_{\mu}\rightarrow\nu_{e}) \approx \sin^{2}2\theta_{13} \sin^{2}\theta_{23} sin^{2}\left( 1.27 \Delta m^{2}_{13} \frac {L}{E}\right)
\label{eq:p_numu_nue}
\end{equation}
it is claimed in our papers \cite{NuePaper} that 
{\em
``For non-maximal $sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$, the confidence intervals remain unchanged to first order by replacing $sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$ by $2sin^{2}\theta_{23}sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$.''}. 
But two difficulties arise with this rescaling of $P(\nu_{\mu}\rightarrow\nu_{e})$ by $2\sin^{2}\theta_{23}$. 
One difficulty is the unknown octant of $\theta_{23}$. 
Since this mixing angle appears in Eq.\ \ref{eq:p_numu_nue} as $\sin^{2}\theta_{23}$, 
a solution in the second octant ($\sin^{2}\theta_{23}>$ 0.5) would move the contours to the right  
while a solution in the first octant ($\sin^{2}\theta_{23}<$ 0.5) would move the contours to the left. 
The second difficulty is that Eq.\ \ref{eq:p_numu_nue} is only a first-order approximation and the effect of the subdominant terms could be important. 
{\color{red} In Fig.\ref{fig:comparisoncontoursrescaling} one can see that there exists actually a remaining difference between the contours obtained 
with maximal prior after re-scaling them (dashed lines), and the contours obtained with the actual true value as prior (solid lines). This plot shows 
the same contours with stand-alone analysis presented in Fig.\ \ref{fig:effectpriornuecontours} re-scaling the contours for maximal prior $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ 
= 1.0 with the cited replacement of $\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ by 2$\sin^{2}\theta_{23}$$\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}$, using for $\theta_{23}$ the true value for the 
toy experiment ($\sin^{2}2\theta_{23}$ = 0.96).}

